format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-and-the-body

lesson risk-in-perspective
title: Risk in perspective
minutes: 6
xp: 50
teaches: u4-three-questions
teaches: u4-read-a-number
relies_on: u4-everyone-dose
relies_on: u4-small-spread

description:
    You will learn three questions to ask about any radiation number, then use them on headlines that sound frightening.

cite ref-epa-radiation-sources
supports: The average yearly dose is about 6.2 millisieverts and a whole body CT is about 10 millisieverts.

cite ref-who-ionizing-radiation

supports:
    Acute radiation syndrome starts at about 1 sievert (1000 millisieverts); a low dose, or one spread over time, carries less risk.

cite ref-epa-radiation-health-effects
supports: Low levels do not cause immediate health effects but can add a small increase in the risk of cancer over a lifetime.

card c01
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Three questions
figure: headline-questions
figure_at: 1

body:
    A headline about radiation is only useful with three questions. How big is the dose, in millisieverts? How fast did it arrive? And how does it compare with doses you already know?

